<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Almanzor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 938–<a href="/years/1002">1002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as al-Mansur ibn Abi Amir, Muhammad ibn Abi Amir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Almanzor (al-Mansur, 'the Victorious'), born Muhammad ibn Abi Amir, was the hajib (chief minister) and real ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> from 978, governing for the young caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a>. He led dozens of campaigns against the Christian north, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 985 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in 997. His sons' failure after 1008 led to the caliphate's collapse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almanzor.jpg" alt="Almanzor" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Txo assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busto_de_Almanzor_en_Calata%C3%B1azor_(cropped)_3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
